      ":question/worked-solution": "Based on the factored equation, we can make the following conclusions about the graph:\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the roots $x=0$ is $4,$ which is even, the graph just touches the $x$-axis and reverses direction at this point.\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=-1$ is $1,$ which is odd, the graph must cross the $x$-axis at this point. Moreover, because $x=-1$ is a simple root, the graph must *not* be tangent to the $x$-axis at this point.\n\n- Since the multiplicity of the root $x=2$ is $3,$ which is odd, the graph must cross the $x$-axis at this point. Moreover, because $x=2$ is a triple-root, the graph must be tangent to the $x$-axis at this point.\n\nOnly the graphs $\\text{III}$ and $\\text{IV}$ satisfy these conditions.\n\nTo choose between these two graphs, we need to consider the end behavior. Since the leading coefficient is negative, as $x$ approaches $\\infty ,$ $y$ approaches $-\\infty .$\n\nOnly graph $\\text{III}$ displays this end behavior. Therefore, the correct answer is $\\text{III}.$",
